\documentclass{article}
\usepackage{graphicx}
\title{Digital Access and Response Behaviour in Household Surveys}
\author{L. Ferreira and P. Ndiaye}
\date{}
\begin{document}
\maketitle

\section{Introduction}
Household surveys increasingly mix face-to-face and online response
modes, and the mix changes who answers quickly and who answers at all.
This note reports two descriptive results from the 2025 wave of the
regional household panel: how response latency relates to education,
and how internet adoption differs across the four survey regions.

\section{Fieldwork}
Interviewers visited 1{,}240 households between March and June 2025.
Each household could complete the questionnaire on paper, by phone, or
online; the mode was recorded together with the time between first
contact and completed interview, which we call the response latency.

\section{Results}
Figure~\ref{fig:latency} plots the response latency of each completed
interview against the respondent's years of education, and
Figure~\ref{fig:adoption} summarises household internet adoption in the
four survey regions.
\begin{figure}
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.75\linewidth]{example-image-a}
\caption{Scatter plot of response latency against years of education.}
\label{fig:latency}
\end{figure}
\begin{figure}
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.75\linewidth]{example-image-b}
\caption{Bar chart of household internet adoption by region.}
\label{fig:adoption}
\end{figure}
Two patterns stand out. Latency falls steadily with education up to
sixteen years of schooling and then flattens, which is consistent with
the online mode being taken up mainly by the more educated households.
Adoption, meanwhile, is near-universal in the metropolitan region but
drops below half of households in the northern districts, so mode mix
there remains dominated by paper and phone.

\section{Discussion}
The regional gap in adoption matters for survey design: offering an
online-first protocol in the northern districts would lengthen fieldwork
rather than shorten it. We therefore keep the mixed-mode design for the
2026 wave and revisit the question once the adoption curve catches up.

\section{Conclusion}
Response latency is strongly patterned by education, and internet
adoption remains uneven across regions. Both facts argue for keeping
the mixed-mode protocol and for weighting the online subsample in any
pooled analysis.

\end{document}

